      "message": "I was pretty confused reading this - I was worried we were migrating something one way, then migrating back again, which would be a sticky situation - but I think I now understand.\n\nThere\u0027s two kinds of data: global data and per-user data.\n\nThe old code has no provision for reading and writing the global data from anywhere except `/data/system_de/usagestats/globalcomponentusage`. The old code *does* have a provision to migrate per-user data from `/data/system/usagestats/10` (for user 10) to `/data/system_ce/10/usagestats`. So I guess things go in the following order:\n\n- write per-user data to `/data/system/usagestats/10`\n- write per-user data to `/data/system_ce/10/usagestats` (and migrate if needed), write global data to `/data/system_de/usagestats/globalcomponentusage`\n- write per-user data to `/data/system_ce/10/usagestats` (and migrate if needed), write global data to `/data/system/usagestats/globalcomponentusage` (and migrate if needed)\n\nThus in particular both global data and per-user data only get migrated once. `/data/system/usagestats` is the \"legacy\" dir for per-user data, but the \"new and better\" dir for global data.\n\nCan we put something like the above in the commit message?",
